<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persian Constitutional Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Iranian Constitutional Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Persian Constitutional Revolution (1905–1911) was a movement of merchants, clergy and reformers that forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a> to grant a constitution and an elected parliament, the Majlis, in 1906. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>'s bombardment of the Majlis in 1908 led to civil war and his deposition in 1909, but Russian intervention closed parliament in 1911. The constitution it created formally remained in force until 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution.jpg" alt="Persian Constitutional Revolution" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sattarkhan_%26_Bagherkhan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> suffered from foreign intrusion, military weakness and corruption. Under the treaties of Gulistan in 1813 and Turkmenchay in 1828 it lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and its Caspian navy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and it had no standing army. The protests began in 1905 against a Belgian director of customs who collected tariffs to repay a Russian loan that had financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a>'s tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Bazaar merchants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> and radicals argued that the shah was selling the country's assets to foreigners to pay the interest on his debts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In December 1905 two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> merchants were bastinadoed for price-gouging, and the bazaar closed in protest. The clergy joined the merchants, drawing on the alliance formed during the Tobacco Protest, and on 12 January 1906 the shah agreed to dismiss his prime minister and create a house of justice. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> killed 22 protesters, about 12,000 men camped through the summer of 1906 in the gardens of the British embassy, demanding a parliament. The shah agreed in August; one hundred and fifty-six members, mostly Tehran merchants, were elected, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">National Consultative Assembly</a> first met in October 1906.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah Qajar</a>, who came to the throne in January 1907, opposed the constitution, and in August that year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> into a Russian zone in the north and a British zone in the south. His coup of 1908 began the period known as the Minor Tyranny, and the cleric Fazlollah Nouri went over to his side. Constitutionalists held out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> and Bagher Khan, and with the support of the Bakhtiyari tribal leaders they marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1909, forced the shah to abdicate in favour of his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-qajar">Ahmad Shah Qajar</a> and restored the constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Nouri was hanged as a traitor in July 1909. In December 1911 the deputies of the Second Majlis were expelled by the shah's cabinet, backed by 12,000 Russian troops, after parliament had appointed the American lawyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morgan-shuster">William Morgan Shuster</a> as treasurer-general. The revolution has been called the first of its kind in the Islamic world, and it opened the way for a lively press. Its legacy was disputed: Ayatollah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> later described the constitution as the work of agents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Persian Constitutional Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Constitutional Revolution</b> transformed the system: in 1906 Mozaffar ad-Din Shah granted a constitution and an elected parliament, the Majlis, and the Supplementary Fundamental Law of 1907 established a constitutional monarchy with guarantees of rights, although the clergy were given a role in reviewing legislation for its conformity…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany Clara Zetkin built the social-democratic women’s movement; in 1899 Qasim Amin, called the “father” of Arab feminism, wrote The Liberation of Women; in 1923 Hoda Shaarawi founded the Egyptian Feminist Union; and the <b>Iranian Constitutional Revolution</b> of 1905 set off an Iranian women’s movement.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>It was made in the early 20th century in the wake of the <b>Iranian Constitutional Revolution</b>, and it has often been sung in protest against injustice, dictatorship and tyranny in Iran; it is often called the &quot;anthem&quot; of Iran's struggles for freedom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/morq-e-sahar">Morq-e Sahar (Bird of the Dawn)</a></li></ul></section>
